.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-COPY-APPLICATION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-copy-application \- define an application again in another namespace
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap copy-application
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-to\-namespace | \-t
.I NAMESPACE
[\fB\-\-as\fR \fINAME\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Defines the same application again in another namespace, leaving the
original alone and running. This is how a build is promoted \- development
to integration, integration to production \- without taking the namespace
it came from out of service.
.PP
To move an application rather than copy it, use
.B \-\-namespace
on
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1).
That takes the definition with it, so what ran in the old namespace stops
running there.
.PP
The copy runs the same artifact: bucket, object key and checksum are taken
as they stand rather than resolved again, so it is the same bytes and not a
rebuild that happens to share a version number.
.PP
It is given its own runtime name and its own technical principal with its
own access key. Both are installation-wide and cannot be shared \- two
applications cannot hold the same data directory, unix socket or log
channel \- so the copy is independent of the original, and revoking one
set of credentials leaves the other running. An application told to run as
a named user keeps that user, which is yours to manage rather than EAP's
to duplicate.
.PP
What the application may reach is re-resolved rather than copied. A bucket
or queue ERN carries the namespace it was resolved in, so copying the list
verbatim would point the new application at the old namespace's data \- a
production application reading development's queues, or the reverse. The
same
.I names
are looked up in the target namespace instead.
.PP
The copy is created stopped, whatever the original is doing, and is started
with
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1).
A copy that started itself would put a second consumer on the target
namespace's queues the moment the command returned.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-application\-id ", " \-n " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application to copy, in the namespace the session is working
in.
.TP
.BR \-\-to\-namespace ", " \-t " " \fINAMESPACE\fR
Namespace to copy it into. It has to exist already \- see
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-namespace (1).
.TP
.B \-\-as " " \fINAME\fR
Name the copy is defined under. The original's name unless given, which is
usually what is wanted; naming it is how an application is copied beside
itself within one namespace.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Non-zero if the application does not exist in the current namespace, if the
target namespace does not exist, if an application of that name is already
defined there, or if a bucket or queue the original may use has no
counterpart of the same name in the target namespace. That last one fails
the copy rather than quietly producing an application with less access than
the one it was copied from.
.SH EXAMPLES
Promote an application to production:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap copy-application --application-id inbox --to-namespace production
euclid-cli eap start-application --application-id inbox
.fi
.RE
.PP
Copy it beside itself to try a change:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap copy-application --application-id inbox --to-namespace development --as inbox-next
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-get-application (1)
